  data:  # 999f
    kind: work_item  # 999f
    title: "python receiver typing is constructor-keyed, but sqlite3/asyncio/multiprocessing receivers come from FACTORY functions (sqlite3.connect, conn.cursor, open_connection)"  # 999f
    status: todo_hard  # 999f
    priority: 2  # 999f
    parent:  # 999f
    tags:  # 999f
    - taint  # 999f
    - io_boundary  # 999f
    - analysis_quality  # 999f
    isbefore: []  # 999f
    duplicate_of: []  # 999f
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 999f
    pr_ref:  # 999f
    description: "THE FINDING THAT MATTERS MORE THAN THE FIX IT CAME FROM. Deriving\nEXTERNAL_CONSTRUCTOR_TYPES from io_primitives (PR pending) moved the generated\nreach probe from 83/215 unreachable python primitives to 5/215. Real code moved\nby ONE edge across two repos, and that one was not even the intended win.\n\n  scripts/measure-catalogue-reach.py python   (generated call sites, CEILING)\n      BOTH     105 -> 153      NEITHER  83 -> 5\n  scripts/measure-ctor-table-derivation-ab.py  (real repos, analyzer-level)\n      hypergumbo  99,470 -> 99,470 call edges   gained 0   lost 0\n      pretix      62,272 -> 62,273              gained 1   lost 0\n\nWHY THE CEILING AND THE FLOOR DISAGREE BY TWO ORDERS OF MAGNITUDE. The probe\ngenerates `sqlite3.Cursor(a).execute(b)`. Real code writes:\n\n      conn = sqlite3.connect(path)      # FACTORY FUNCTION, not the class\n      cur  = conn.cursor()              # FACTORY METHOD on a typed receiver\n      cur.execute(sql)                  # the sink\n\nhypergumbo's own tracker cache is exactly this shape (packages/hypergumbo-tracker\n/src/hypergumbo_tracker/cache.py:93 `sqlite3.connect(str(self._db_path))`), and\nit is why hypergumbo gained zero. The constructor table keys on the TYPE NAME, so\nit can never fire for a type whose idiomatic construction is a factory.\n\nSPLIT OF THE 15 TYPES BY HOW THEY ARE ACTUALLY OBTAINED (read from stdlib usage,\nnot measured on a corpus -- treat as a hypothesis with a named next step):\n  constructor-shaped, so already fixed:  flask.Flask, ftplib.FTP, smtplib.SMTP,\n      http.client.HTTPConnection/HTTPSConnection, http.server.HTTPServer,\n      socketserver.TCPServer, xmlrpc.server.SimpleXMLRPCServer,\n      multiprocessing.Queue\n  FACTORY-shaped, so still unreachable: sqlite3.Connection (sqlite3.connect),\n      sqlite3.Cursor (conn.cursor()), asyncio.StreamReader/StreamWriter\n      (asyncio.open_connection), multiprocessing.Pipe\n  not constructed at all: django.db.models\n\nWHAT WOULD ACTUALLY CLOSE IT. A factory map -- callee qualified-name -> returned\nreceiver type -- consulted at the same seam as the constructor table. The\ncatalogue does NOT already carry this: io_primitives records which METHODS are\nI/O, never which FUNCTIONS return the receiving type, so unlike the constructor\ntable this cannot be derived and needs its own declared data. That is the reason\nto file it rather than fold it into the constructor work.\n\nMEASURE FIRST, and the instrument already exists: extend\nmeasure-catalogue-reach.py with a factory form per type and re-run the real-repo\nA/B. Do not build the map before the corpus says the shape is there -- this item\nexists precisely because a ceiling was mistaken for a payoff once already."  # 999f
    fields: {}  # 999f
