- op: discuss  # 456c
  at: '2026-08-10T21:27:29Z'  # 456c
  by: agent  # 456c
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 456c
  clock: 3  # 456c
  nonce: 456c  # 456c
  message: "MEASURED, AND THE ANSWER IS DO NOT BUILD IT. 2026-08-10, dev 6a1037d98d.\nInstrument in-repo: scripts/measure-factory-receiver-shape.py.\n\nThis item asked for exactly this before any code: \"Do not build the map before\nthe corpus says the shape is there.\" The corpus says it is not.\n\nMETHOD. Ground truth is `ast`, deliberately NOT the analyzer -- the question is\n\"what does real code contain\", and asking hypergumbo would answer \"what can\nhypergumbo see\", which is the thing under test. Import-aware, tracks local vars\nAND `self.<attr>` bindings, splits by production/test with production's own\nis_test_file. Cohort: hypergumbo, pretix, superset, mlflow, litellm, fastapi,\nnova -- 11,674 python files.\n\nPOSITIVE CONTROL FAILED ON ITS FIRST RUN AND THAT IS WHY THE NUMBER IS BELIEVABLE.\nThe scanner tracked only local `Name` bindings, so it could not see\n`self._conn = sqlite3.connect(...)` -- an INSTANCE ATTRIBUTE, which is how a\nlong-lived connection is actually written, and the exact shape of the control\nfile (hypergumbo's own tracker cache). Without the control the corpus would have\nreturned near-zero and this item would have been closed on a scanner bug. Fixed\nby a two-pass class scan; control now finds 3 bindings / 20 catalogued sites.\n\n  shape                                   sites   production\n  CTOR    c = smtplib.SMTP(h); c.sendmail()  192       132     already works (#270)\n  FACTORY c = sqlite3.connect(p); c.execute() 29        21     <- THIS ITEM\n  MANAGER Model.objects.filter()           4,690        --     not modelled at all\n\n*** THE 29 IS SELF-BIASED: 22 OF THEM ARE HYPERGUMBO'S OWN TRACKER CACHE. ***\nExcluding self, the factory shape is SEVEN sites across six repos, of which\nexactly ONE is on a production path (litellm). Per repo, production factory\nsites: pretix 0, superset 0, mlflow 0, litellm 1, fastapi 0, nova 0.\n\nTHE ITEM'S OWN HYPOTHESIS NAMED THREE TYPES; THE CORPUS REFUTES ALL THREE.\n  sqlite3.connect            4 bindings cohort-wide, essentially all self\n  asyncio.open_connection    ZERO occurrences\n  multiprocessing.Pipe       ZERO occurrences\nBindings that DID fire: pathlib.Path 302 (CTOR, already handled),\ntempfile.NamedTemporaryFile 18, socket.socket 9, flask.Flask 6, sqlite3.connect 4.\n\nVERDICT: a hand-written, hand-maintained factory->type map would be declared data\nserving 1 production site outside this repo. That is not worth the maintenance\nsurface. Marking wont_do rather than leaving it open to be re-proposed; the\nmeasurement is the durable output.\n\nWHAT THE SCAN FOUND INSTEAD, and it is 161x larger: the Django MANAGER shape,\n`Model.objects.<method>()`, 4,690 calls in pretix alone against 29 factory sites\ncohort-wide. django.db.models carries 29 catalogued methods -- the largest single\nreceiver type in python.yaml -- and it is neither a constructor nor a factory\ncall but an ATTRIBUTE on a class. Count reconciled against raw grep (4,991\n`.objects.` occurrences; 4,191 for just four of the 29 methods), so it is not a\ndetector artifact. Filed separately.\n\nCAVEATS. One Django app in the cohort, so 4,690 is one repo's number, not a\nlanguage-wide one. FACTORY_RETURNS is a hand-written hypothesis: a missing row\nundercounts, so 29 is a floor for the shape as I modelled it -- but the three\ntypes this item actually named are covered and two of them are literal zeros."  # 456c
- op: discuss  # 298a
  at: '2026-08-10T21:31:16Z'  # 298a
  by: agent  # 298a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 298a
  clock: 4  # 298a
  nonce: 298a  # 298a
  message: "CORRECTION to the note above, same day, before anyone acts on it.\n\nI wrote that the Django MANAGER shape (`Model.objects.<method>()`, 4,690 calls)\nwas \"not modelled at all\". THAT IS FALSE. It is modelled, by WI-sozoj at\npy.py:5734 -- gated on `func.value.attr == \"objects\"` plus a closed\nDJANGO_ORM_MANAGER_METHODS set, emitting a `django.db.models`-qualified dst so\nio-boundary's module-filter path classifies each method db_read/db_write. My AST\ndetector used the same `.objects.` discriminator, which is exactly why the counts\nreconciled -- I reproduced a shipped feature's own rule and then reported it as a\ngap.\n\nMEASURED ON PRETIX (hypergumbo io-boundaries, full cold analysis):\n    db_read 736 chains | db_write 110 | total_io_edges 1,052\n    django-attributed chains: 846\n      filter 447 · get 121 · create 55 · all 54 · select_related 36 ·\n      bulk_create 25 · none 18 · prefetch_related 16 · annotate 15 ·\n      get_or_create 13 · iterator 9 · bulk_update 8 · update_or_create 7 ·\n      exclude 6 · values 5 · count 3 · order_by 3 · exists 2 · in_bulk 1 ·\n      save 1 · delete 1\n    django.db.models.filter is the single largest primitive in the whole repo.\n\nAND I COMPARED TWO POPULATIONS THAT ARE NOT COMPARABLE. 4,690 is a SITE count\nfrom an AST scan; 846 is a CHAIN count, deduplicated per (boundary, primitive,\nsrc), so many call sites in one function collapse to one chain. Reading\n846/4,690 as \"18% coverage\" would be wrong in the same way this project has been\nwrong before -- sites, chains and walks are different populations and are never\ninterchangeable. No coverage ratio is claimed here.\n\nWHAT SURVIVES UNCHANGED: the wont_do verdict on this item. The factory shape is\n7 sites outside this repo with 1 on a production path, and asyncio /\nmultiprocessing are literal zeros. Nothing about the Django correction touches\nthat.\n\nWHAT THE SCAN ACTUALLY CONTRIBUTED, restated honestly: (1) it refutes this\nitem's hypothesis, and (2) it independently confirms that the dominant python\nreceiver shape in a real Django app is already handled. The open question it\nleaves is PRECISION, not recall -- whether the closed method set and the\n`.objects.` guard admit anything they should not -- and that is not measured\nhere and is not filed as a gap on this evidence."  # 298a
